   "html_text": "(This paradox is also known as the paradox of 1001 cats; Geach 1980, Lewis 1993.) Focus on Oscar and Oscar ⟦P⟧ at ⟦M⟧ —before Oscar loses his tail. Is Oscar ⟦P⟧ a dog? When Oscar loses his tail the resulting creature is certainly a dog. Why then should we deny that Oscar ⟦P⟧ is a dog? We saw above that one possible response to Chrysippus’ paradox was to claim that Oscar ⟦P⟧ does not exist from ⟦M⟧ on. But even if we adopt this view, how does it follow that Oscar ⟦P⟧ , existing as it does at ⟦M⟧ ",
